WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.907
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.907 --> 00:00:09.240
NICOLE FORSGREN: Today we are
going to be talking about,

00:00:09.240 --> 00:00:11.040
if you don't know
where you're going,

00:00:11.040 --> 00:00:15.100
it doesn't matter how
fast you get there.

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:19.740
And when I go speak with
companies, and organizations,

00:00:19.740 --> 00:00:24.660
and developers, and
operators, and executives,

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.930
we want to start doing
everything better, and faster,

00:00:27.930 --> 00:00:31.050
and stronger, and more
secure, and more reliable.

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.360
Who's heard this
about a million times?

00:00:33.360 --> 00:00:34.190
Yes.

00:00:34.190 --> 00:00:34.700
OK.

00:00:34.700 --> 00:00:37.070
Who are the devs in the house?

00:00:37.070 --> 00:00:39.470
Who are the ops in the house?

00:00:39.470 --> 00:00:41.740
Who's management and executives?

00:00:41.740 --> 00:00:43.500
Yeah.

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:45.960
We all want to do
the thing, right?

00:00:45.960 --> 00:00:47.820
Because who wants to be awesome?

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:48.900
All of us.

00:00:48.900 --> 00:00:51.680
Who wants to suck at things?

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:52.720
OK, good.

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:53.350
Trick question.

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:55.100
I was like, who's just
raising their hands

00:00:55.100 --> 00:00:56.750
because this is the exercise?

00:00:59.440 --> 00:01:02.530
We want to do everything
better because--

00:01:02.530 --> 00:01:05.980
we got into this because
tech is exciting and fun,

00:01:05.980 --> 00:01:08.410
and we get to make
things, and be creative,

00:01:08.410 --> 00:01:11.890
and watch the lights light
up, and do Hello World,

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:14.500
and make things awesome.

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:18.940
But it's tough when
we aren't necessarily

00:01:18.940 --> 00:01:23.840
sure what that means.

00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:27.140
Except also, sometimes that's
great, because we understand,

00:01:27.140 --> 00:01:31.400
and we're the experts, and
we know what that looks like.

00:01:31.400 --> 00:01:36.320
So how is it that we do this?

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:39.500
So that's what we get
to talk about today.

00:01:39.500 --> 00:01:41.820
Where are we going?

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.660
How do we actually
improve performance?

00:01:44.660 --> 00:01:46.490
And how tricky is that word?

00:01:46.490 --> 00:01:48.750
Performance is a tough one.

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:51.380
How do we make it a carrot
and not just a stick?

00:01:51.380 --> 00:01:52.670
How should we measure it?

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:55.560
Why should we actually care?

00:01:55.560 --> 00:01:58.160
And then at the end, what
is this culture thing?

00:01:58.160 --> 00:01:59.930
And how do we
actually measure it

00:01:59.930 --> 00:02:03.290
so that we can then
make it better?

00:02:03.290 --> 00:02:07.230
So let's start with,
where are we going?

00:02:07.230 --> 00:02:08.740
Who here's heard
of maturity models?

00:02:08.740 --> 00:02:11.220
Do we know what
maturity models are?

00:02:11.220 --> 00:02:11.982
I'm so excited.

00:02:11.982 --> 00:02:13.440
Who's not heard of
maturity models?

00:02:13.440 --> 00:02:15.060
Do we want a real quick recap?

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.250
Do we want a recap anyway?

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:17.750
OK.

00:02:17.750 --> 00:02:19.910
So maturity models are
this wonderful thing

00:02:19.910 --> 00:02:24.830
that help explain where we
are, and they're usually

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.852
like predefined levels.

00:02:26.852 --> 00:02:28.310
By the way, they're
usually made up

00:02:28.310 --> 00:02:30.470
by someone trying to
sell us something.

00:02:30.470 --> 00:02:31.700
They all go to level five.

00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:33.825
Do we know why they got to
level five, most of them

00:02:33.825 --> 00:02:34.910
go to level five?

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:37.790
Because we all
have five fingers.

00:02:37.790 --> 00:02:38.900
It's true.

00:02:38.900 --> 00:02:41.480
It's true.

00:02:41.480 --> 00:02:44.480
Who here has played
"World of Warcraft?"

00:02:44.480 --> 00:02:44.980
Fine.

00:02:44.980 --> 00:02:47.960
Who here has a friend who's
played "World of Warcraft?"

00:02:47.960 --> 00:02:48.790
Yes.

00:02:48.790 --> 00:02:49.570
Here we go.

00:02:49.570 --> 00:02:54.083
When "World of Warcraft" first
came out, this was the land.

00:02:54.083 --> 00:02:56.500
So even if you haven' tread,
even if all your friends have

00:02:56.500 --> 00:02:57.910
played, we all get this.

00:02:57.910 --> 00:02:59.590
It's a map.

00:02:59.590 --> 00:03:02.710
When it first came out,
it went to level 60.

00:03:02.710 --> 00:03:08.050
You could create a
bunch of characters.

00:03:08.050 --> 00:03:09.850
Clearly, I've played
"World of Warcraft."

00:03:09.850 --> 00:03:11.520
Clearly, I had to stop
playing "World of Warcraft"

00:03:11.520 --> 00:03:13.800
because I had to finish
work, and school, and jobs,

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.340
and stuff.

00:03:15.340 --> 00:03:18.940
You could be a mage or
all this cool stuff.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.427
It went to level 60.

00:03:20.427 --> 00:03:21.760
You could have a bunch of tools.

00:03:21.760 --> 00:03:24.850
You could have a
bunch of technologies.

00:03:24.850 --> 00:03:27.067
So let's create ourselves
a maturity model.

00:03:27.067 --> 00:03:28.150
How do we feel about this?

00:03:31.408 --> 00:03:32.700
Let's pretend we're executives.

00:03:32.700 --> 00:03:33.950
Let's create a maturity model.

00:03:33.950 --> 00:03:35.300
How do we feel about this?

00:03:35.300 --> 00:03:37.710
Yay.

00:03:37.710 --> 00:03:39.035
Y'all are my people.

00:03:39.035 --> 00:03:40.775
This is great.

00:03:40.775 --> 00:03:42.150
But we don't have
very much time,

00:03:42.150 --> 00:03:45.570
so our maturity models are only
going to go to level three.

00:03:45.570 --> 00:03:49.590
So the first level is
going to be level good.

00:03:49.590 --> 00:03:52.960
And we're going to like
conquer land on the left.

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.220
The second maturity model
is going to be level--

00:03:56.220 --> 00:03:59.180
and we'll go to level 40.

00:03:59.180 --> 00:04:03.660
The second level is
going to be level great,

00:04:03.660 --> 00:04:05.638
and it'll be all of
the land on the left,

00:04:05.638 --> 00:04:06.930
and half the land on the right.

00:04:06.930 --> 00:04:09.715
And we'll go to level 50.

00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:14.640
The third level will be
called level amazing,

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.519
and we will conquer
all of the lands.

00:04:17.519 --> 00:04:19.070
Sound good?

00:04:19.070 --> 00:04:19.570
Yay.

00:04:19.570 --> 00:04:20.070
OK.

00:04:20.070 --> 00:04:21.440
So we progress.

00:04:21.440 --> 00:04:22.420
We all move forward.

00:04:22.420 --> 00:04:24.580
We've conquered all the lands.

00:04:24.580 --> 00:04:27.730
And at the end of this
exercise, what happens?

00:04:27.730 --> 00:04:29.620
We get a gold star
on our forehead.

00:04:29.620 --> 00:04:32.480
Everyone, gold star
on our forehead.

00:04:32.480 --> 00:04:34.020
Participate.

00:04:34.020 --> 00:04:35.840
Gold star on our forehead.

00:04:35.840 --> 00:04:38.660
We collect our fat bonus check,
and we pay our consultants.

00:04:38.660 --> 00:04:41.930
Congratulations.

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.012
Now let's fast
forward several years.

00:04:44.012 --> 00:04:44.720
And what happens?

00:04:47.420 --> 00:04:51.052
This is now the land
of "World of Warcraft."

00:04:54.220 --> 00:04:56.200
The land has changed.

00:04:56.200 --> 00:04:57.565
The landscape has changed.

00:05:01.460 --> 00:05:04.970
By the way, you can
now go to level 110.

00:05:04.970 --> 00:05:06.830
The technology has changed.

00:05:06.830 --> 00:05:08.660
The competition has changed.

00:05:08.660 --> 00:05:09.950
Y'all, you can have pets now.

00:05:14.400 --> 00:05:17.370
Does anyone care that we got
our gold star on our forehead

00:05:17.370 --> 00:05:19.500
and we got to level 60?

00:05:19.500 --> 00:05:22.690
If we go out and try to
fight, what's going to happen?

00:05:22.690 --> 00:05:25.990
We're going to get
our butts kicked.

00:05:25.990 --> 00:05:27.790
Our competition has changed.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:30.940
Our customers'
expectations have changed.

00:05:30.940 --> 00:05:34.570
And this is a fun,
silly example and story,

00:05:34.570 --> 00:05:37.360
but it's also very
relevant for technology.

00:05:41.180 --> 00:05:44.640
So the challenge
with maturity models,

00:05:44.640 --> 00:05:48.380
particularly as we talk about
developing and delivering

00:05:48.380 --> 00:05:51.900
technology and
features for customers,

00:05:51.900 --> 00:05:55.610
whether they're internal end
users or external end users,

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:59.165
is that it doesn't help us
actually address what matters.

00:06:04.440 --> 00:06:07.350
They tell us that we're
done when we reach

00:06:07.350 --> 00:06:10.140
a pre-defined destination.

00:06:10.140 --> 00:06:13.800
And the whole rest of the world
keeps addressing and answering

00:06:13.800 --> 00:06:15.380
what matters and
what's important.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:25.280
They also tell us and leaders
to stop dedicating resources

00:06:25.280 --> 00:06:26.440
once we've arrived.

00:06:26.440 --> 00:06:28.880
By the way, resources
aren't just money.

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:30.040
It's time.

00:06:30.040 --> 00:06:31.330
It's attention.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:32.380
It's education.

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:33.880
It's training.

00:06:33.880 --> 00:06:36.790
It's paying attention to what
our customers need and want.

00:06:41.370 --> 00:06:44.840
They also tell us that we all
follow the exact same path

00:06:44.840 --> 00:06:47.093
to success.

00:06:47.093 --> 00:06:49.010
Remember our super fun
made up maturity model?

00:06:49.010 --> 00:06:50.880
It said that we had
to explore that land

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:53.300
in the exact same order.

00:06:53.300 --> 00:06:56.780
Now if we think about this
in terms of technology

00:06:56.780 --> 00:07:01.670
transformation, getting
our teams better, faster

00:07:01.670 --> 00:07:02.870
delivering--

00:07:02.870 --> 00:07:05.120
think about the organizations
that we're in right now.

00:07:05.120 --> 00:07:07.880
Think about all the teams that
are working on and delivering

00:07:07.880 --> 00:07:09.470
technology.

00:07:09.470 --> 00:07:12.060
Do they all look the same?

00:07:12.060 --> 00:07:13.590
Probably not.

00:07:13.590 --> 00:07:16.470
So their next step forward--

00:07:16.470 --> 00:07:19.930
will it look exactly the same?

00:07:19.930 --> 00:07:21.340
Probably not.

00:07:21.340 --> 00:07:22.430
And the next step.

00:07:22.430 --> 00:07:23.300
And the next step.

00:07:23.300 --> 00:07:24.900
And the next step.

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.150
Again, probably not.

00:07:28.150 --> 00:07:31.990
So how do we expect
a step by step

00:07:31.990 --> 00:07:37.360
by step map and
prescriptive path

00:07:37.360 --> 00:07:40.106
to make sense for something
that's so complex?

00:07:43.490 --> 00:07:48.380
Now, maturity models tell us
that technology is a checklist

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:50.415
to just be completed.

00:07:50.415 --> 00:07:52.040
And when we're done,
we're done, and we

00:07:52.040 --> 00:07:54.390
move on to something else.

00:07:54.390 --> 00:07:58.340
But we got into this
game because it's fun,

00:07:58.340 --> 00:08:03.320
and it's exciting, and we get
to keep discovering what's new.

00:08:06.110 --> 00:08:10.370
Now listen, I
fundamentally understand

00:08:10.370 --> 00:08:14.040
why maturity models are good,
and why they're comforting.

00:08:14.040 --> 00:08:21.200
It's because it lets me
get that someone else has

00:08:21.200 --> 00:08:24.310
been where I've been, and that
they know what comes next.

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:27.923
But if they point me
too far into the future,

00:08:27.923 --> 00:08:28.715
that's not helpful.

00:08:32.260 --> 00:08:33.280
Thanks, Nicole.

00:08:33.280 --> 00:08:35.260
You just brought down the room.

00:08:35.260 --> 00:08:36.669
Sad trombone.

00:08:36.669 --> 00:08:38.860
So how do we actually get there?

00:08:38.860 --> 00:08:40.950
What's helpful?

00:08:40.950 --> 00:08:43.230
What's helpful is to
focus on a constraint.

00:08:43.230 --> 00:08:48.890
By constraint, I basically just
mean, what's holding me back?

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:53.290
What's the hurdle for each team?

00:08:53.290 --> 00:09:00.170
Focus on that, fix it,
execute well and completely.

00:09:00.170 --> 00:09:01.660
I make it sound simple.

00:09:01.660 --> 00:09:02.160
It's not.

00:09:02.160 --> 00:09:03.840
I know it's a challenge.

00:09:03.840 --> 00:09:08.940
But that's also why we
rely on our expertise,

00:09:08.940 --> 00:09:12.277
and on our experts, and
also on our community.

00:09:12.277 --> 00:09:14.610
One thing I really really
love about the DevOps movement

00:09:14.610 --> 00:09:17.220
that I've seen over
the last decade

00:09:17.220 --> 00:09:21.910
is that we really rely on
each other to move forward.

00:09:21.910 --> 00:09:25.030
OK, so then where are we going?

00:09:25.030 --> 00:09:27.400
A direction is helpful.

00:09:27.400 --> 00:09:30.910
I didn't say we
have to fly blind.

00:09:30.910 --> 00:09:35.350
We should know at least what
direction we should be facing.

00:09:35.350 --> 00:09:37.210
OK, so then what
direction is that?

00:09:37.210 --> 00:09:39.730
And the number one
question I get so often

00:09:39.730 --> 00:09:43.480
is, is there one
metric that matters?

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:45.980
Can anyone guess the
right answer here?

00:09:45.980 --> 00:09:46.790
No.

00:09:46.790 --> 00:09:48.030
Sorry.

00:09:48.030 --> 00:09:50.570
Go ahead and tell
your executives that.

00:09:50.570 --> 00:09:53.370
No, there's not.

00:09:53.370 --> 00:09:56.920
But if I have to pick
something, this would be it.

00:09:56.920 --> 00:09:58.670
It would be software
delivery performance.

00:09:58.670 --> 00:10:03.590
And it's not one metric,
but it is four of them.

00:10:03.590 --> 00:10:06.573
And they tend to
hang out together.

00:10:06.573 --> 00:10:08.240
They all go drink tea
together, and they

00:10:08.240 --> 00:10:10.830
have a little knitting
circle, and they chill,

00:10:10.830 --> 00:10:11.705
and they throw darts.

00:10:14.890 --> 00:10:17.740
Here's what I mean when I say
software delivery performance.

00:10:17.740 --> 00:10:20.320
I mean some throughput
and stability measures.

00:10:20.320 --> 00:10:22.420
Throughput is speed.

00:10:22.420 --> 00:10:24.580
So I'm talking about
deployment frequency--

00:10:24.580 --> 00:10:27.235
how often you push code.

00:10:27.235 --> 00:10:30.490
I'm also talking about
lead time for changes--

00:10:30.490 --> 00:10:34.870
CodeCommit to code
running and production.

00:10:34.870 --> 00:10:37.300
And then I'm talking
about stability.

00:10:37.300 --> 00:10:41.760
Stability is time to restore
service and change fail rate.

00:10:41.760 --> 00:10:43.740
Now, someone challenged
me earlier this week,

00:10:43.740 --> 00:10:45.365
and I really appreciate
her doing that.

00:10:45.365 --> 00:10:47.730
Because she said, why
are you only focusing

00:10:47.730 --> 00:10:49.950
on things that are
easy to measure?

00:10:49.950 --> 00:10:52.630
Now really quickly, I'd say
these aren't necessarily easy.

00:10:52.630 --> 00:10:54.220
But she had a really
fantastic point.

00:10:54.220 --> 00:10:56.460
Because there are so many
things that are important,

00:10:56.460 --> 00:10:58.980
and why are you
focusing on this?

00:10:58.980 --> 00:11:01.230
I'm not focusing on
this at the exclusion

00:11:01.230 --> 00:11:04.780
of other things, like culture,
and people, and quality.

00:11:04.780 --> 00:11:06.640
But this is important
for a few things.

00:11:06.640 --> 00:11:08.910
First of all, this
focuses on the performance

00:11:08.910 --> 00:11:11.250
of software delivery.

00:11:11.250 --> 00:11:16.520
And notice that I'm measuring
things that are intention.

00:11:16.520 --> 00:11:18.620
So often people have
told us that we can't

00:11:18.620 --> 00:11:20.378
have both speed and stability.

00:11:20.378 --> 00:11:22.670
Has anyone heard that, for
things to be stable or safe,

00:11:22.670 --> 00:11:25.070
we have to slow down?

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:26.240
We've heard this.

00:11:26.240 --> 00:11:29.270
It's been whispered
in dark alleys.

00:11:29.270 --> 00:11:32.900
We've found over five
years of research

00:11:32.900 --> 00:11:38.750
and 30,000 data points,
these actually move together.

00:11:38.750 --> 00:11:40.250
And when I say move
together, I mean

00:11:40.250 --> 00:11:41.958
I take all these data
points-- do we want

00:11:41.958 --> 00:11:43.850
a stats tangent really quick?

00:11:43.850 --> 00:11:45.250
Can I [INAUDIBLE] for a second?

00:11:45.250 --> 00:11:45.780
OK.

00:11:45.780 --> 00:11:46.582
Yes, OK.

00:11:46.582 --> 00:11:49.040
I take all the data points,
and I throw them in the hopper.

00:11:49.040 --> 00:11:51.560
A very technical term--
throw it in the hopper.

00:11:51.560 --> 00:11:53.420
And I do a cluster
analysis, which

00:11:53.420 --> 00:11:56.360
means I want to see how
they cluster together.

00:11:56.360 --> 00:11:58.660
So I see them all
grouped together

00:11:58.660 --> 00:12:01.400
in statistically meaningful
ways, and then a gap.

00:12:01.400 --> 00:12:03.710
So they're statistically
different from another group.

00:12:03.710 --> 00:12:06.245
And then a group, and then
a gap, and then a group.

00:12:08.790 --> 00:12:10.220
I'm real creative,
so I call them

00:12:10.220 --> 00:12:13.580
high performers, and medium
performers, and low performers.

00:12:13.580 --> 00:12:16.700
But what that means, when
they group together, I'm

00:12:16.700 --> 00:12:20.250
seeing the speed and stability
measures move together.

00:12:20.250 --> 00:12:23.150
The high performers are
doing everything well.

00:12:23.150 --> 00:12:27.290
The medium performers--
we're seeing the middle group

00:12:27.290 --> 00:12:28.360
hang out together.

00:12:28.360 --> 00:12:30.050
And some people are
on the struggle bus

00:12:30.050 --> 00:12:33.080
together down at the bottom.

00:12:33.080 --> 00:12:35.940
We see movement together.

00:12:35.940 --> 00:12:38.700
There aren't trade offs.

00:12:38.700 --> 00:12:40.270
I think that's important.

00:12:40.270 --> 00:12:43.710
So if you walk away with only
one thing, have it be that.

00:12:43.710 --> 00:12:45.675
Speed and stability
are possible together.

00:12:51.190 --> 00:12:53.620
So then what's next?

00:12:53.620 --> 00:12:55.735
Some people wonder
where they might fall.

00:12:55.735 --> 00:12:56.610
This is my eye chart.

00:12:56.610 --> 00:12:58.410
Sorry it's so small.

00:12:58.410 --> 00:13:01.650
This comes from
the 2018 Accelerate

00:13:01.650 --> 00:13:03.270
State of DevOps report.

00:13:03.270 --> 00:13:07.830
So when we take this very
data driven approach,

00:13:07.830 --> 00:13:10.030
where do people fall?

00:13:10.030 --> 00:13:11.190
We see elite performers.

00:13:11.190 --> 00:13:15.240
So this is the highest group.

00:13:15.240 --> 00:13:19.830
The high performers can
push code on demand.

00:13:19.830 --> 00:13:21.300
Lead time for changes--

00:13:21.300 --> 00:13:24.690
again CodeCommit to code
writing and production--

00:13:24.690 --> 00:13:26.240
less than an hour.

00:13:26.240 --> 00:13:28.980
It's getting it all the
way through your pipeline.

00:13:28.980 --> 00:13:30.400
Time to restore service--

00:13:30.400 --> 00:13:31.930
again, less than an hour.

00:13:31.930 --> 00:13:34.950
Do you know what makes me
super excited about that?

00:13:34.950 --> 00:13:37.410
That means that when
you have an emergency--

00:13:37.410 --> 00:13:41.880
something breaks-- your regular
process looks very, very much

00:13:41.880 --> 00:13:43.590
like your emergency process.

00:13:43.590 --> 00:13:45.720
You're not skipping tests.

00:13:45.720 --> 00:13:47.610
Everything goes through.

00:13:47.610 --> 00:13:50.228
Change fail rate-- it's
between 0% and 15%.

00:13:50.228 --> 00:13:52.020
And by the way,
historically, that actually

00:13:52.020 --> 00:13:55.290
looks very close to
the 2%, 3%, 5% mark.

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:57.460
Compare that to
our low performers.

00:13:57.460 --> 00:13:58.700
These are median numbers.

00:13:58.700 --> 00:14:01.380
So we do have a range here.

00:14:01.380 --> 00:14:05.520
We see deploy frequency between
once a week and once a month,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:09.990
lead time for changes between
one month and six months, time

00:14:09.990 --> 00:14:13.020
to restore service between
one week and one month.

00:14:13.020 --> 00:14:16.380
This is where we're
seeing a difference.

00:14:16.380 --> 00:14:20.280
And then our change fail
rate is between 46% and 60%.

00:14:20.280 --> 00:14:22.320
Now, if you take a
look at this, you

00:14:22.320 --> 00:14:24.510
think about where
your team might fall.

00:14:24.510 --> 00:14:27.730
If you don't see
yourself on this chart,

00:14:27.730 --> 00:14:29.008
don't be discouraged.

00:14:29.008 --> 00:14:31.300
So often I'll get people
contacting me and they'll say,

00:14:31.300 --> 00:14:32.530
but I'm not here.

00:14:32.530 --> 00:14:33.520
This isn't my industry.

00:14:33.520 --> 00:14:34.460
It doesn't apply.

00:14:34.460 --> 00:14:37.510
First of all, all
industries are represented.

00:14:37.510 --> 00:14:40.720
Highly regulated fields
show up in elite performers.

00:14:40.720 --> 00:14:44.470
Crazy cowboy startups-- low
performers were everywhere.

00:14:44.470 --> 00:14:46.480
But don't be discouraged.

00:14:46.480 --> 00:14:50.770
Because everyone can
achieve high performance.

00:14:50.770 --> 00:14:52.990
We just need to start working.

00:14:52.990 --> 00:14:55.095
And everyone can do it.

00:14:55.095 --> 00:14:56.720
We can all work
together and get there.

00:14:59.960 --> 00:15:02.090
But how?

00:15:02.090 --> 00:15:02.930
How do we get there?

00:15:02.930 --> 00:15:06.000
One does not just walk
into high performance.

00:15:06.000 --> 00:15:07.000
So how do we get there?

00:15:09.650 --> 00:15:13.020
DevOps, or whatever
you want to call

00:15:13.020 --> 00:15:16.110
it-- technology transformation,
whatever your leadership

00:15:16.110 --> 00:15:19.880
team likes, just do your
little find and replace.

00:15:19.880 --> 00:15:23.180
The cross-functional
community dedicated

00:15:23.180 --> 00:15:28.820
to building, evolving, and
operating secure, resilient

00:15:28.820 --> 00:15:29.790
systems at scale.

00:15:33.290 --> 00:15:36.998
It can include so many job
titles, tasks, and roles.

00:15:36.998 --> 00:15:38.540
But I think the part
that's important

00:15:38.540 --> 00:15:40.220
is that we work together.

00:15:40.220 --> 00:15:41.600
We automate.

00:15:41.600 --> 00:15:43.100
We reduce toil.

00:15:43.100 --> 00:15:46.040
And we deliver value for
whoever our end users are.

00:15:49.070 --> 00:15:51.840
So this is a summary of my
research over the last five

00:15:51.840 --> 00:15:52.340
years.

00:15:55.010 --> 00:15:57.310
And right now,
everyone is like, what?

00:16:01.110 --> 00:16:03.030
Girl.

00:16:03.030 --> 00:16:05.350
So let's think of it this way.

00:16:05.350 --> 00:16:07.350
We have a cultural aspect.

00:16:07.350 --> 00:16:09.870
We have a tooling and
automation aspect.

00:16:09.870 --> 00:16:11.730
And we have some process.

00:16:11.730 --> 00:16:14.475
It really breaks down
into these things.

00:16:17.910 --> 00:16:19.440
We can manage this.

00:16:19.440 --> 00:16:22.060
Do we feel OK?

00:16:22.060 --> 00:16:25.680
Have we heard
culture is important?

00:16:25.680 --> 00:16:28.215
We can do culture.

00:16:28.215 --> 00:16:29.340
We're at a tech conference.

00:16:29.340 --> 00:16:31.465
Tell me that we've heard
that tooling is important.

00:16:31.465 --> 00:16:32.640
OK, we're good here.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.400
And process.

00:16:35.400 --> 00:16:37.270
OK, now we're back.

00:16:37.270 --> 00:16:39.570
Here's what we see.

00:16:39.570 --> 00:16:41.970
So technical practices and
continuous delivery-- that's

00:16:41.970 --> 00:16:42.690
at the bottom.

00:16:42.690 --> 00:16:43.800
We're going to break this down.

00:16:43.800 --> 00:16:45.342
By the way, anytime
you see an arrow,

00:16:45.342 --> 00:16:48.100
this is a predictive
relationship.

00:16:48.100 --> 00:16:50.290
Lean management and lean
product development--

00:16:50.290 --> 00:16:53.135
these are going to
be some processes.

00:16:53.135 --> 00:16:55.510
This arrow-- see this SDO
performance-- that's our speed,

00:16:55.510 --> 00:16:57.720
and stability, and availability.

00:16:57.720 --> 00:17:00.480
So all of these lead
into this stuff.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.130
A quick tangent in the
middle-- outsourcing is bad.

00:17:03.130 --> 00:17:03.970
Who's surprised?

00:17:07.900 --> 00:17:09.550
Cloud infrastructure helps.

00:17:09.550 --> 00:17:14.420
That's a huge driver,
if you do it right.

00:17:14.420 --> 00:17:16.550
The things I love
about culture--

00:17:16.550 --> 00:17:19.369
we can see, autonomy
helps drive culture.

00:17:19.369 --> 00:17:21.890
Western organizational
culture, we'll talk about.

00:17:21.890 --> 00:17:23.930
It drives job satisfaction.

00:17:23.930 --> 00:17:26.160
Look down here at the bottom.

00:17:26.160 --> 00:17:30.470
Identity, if we
really love our work.

00:17:30.470 --> 00:17:32.860
Smart investments.

00:17:32.860 --> 00:17:34.050
Decreased burnout.

00:17:34.050 --> 00:17:36.500
Decreased deployment pain.

00:17:36.500 --> 00:17:38.980
Decreased rework.

00:17:38.980 --> 00:17:41.230
In SRE land, we call this toil.

00:17:41.230 --> 00:17:43.570
We're decreasing toil.

00:17:43.570 --> 00:17:47.240
And it helps us with
organizational performance.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:50.230
And at the very, very
back, at the very far left,

00:17:50.230 --> 00:17:53.350
we see transformational
leadership.

00:17:53.350 --> 00:17:57.700
Has anyone here had a really,
really great leader, manager,

00:17:57.700 --> 00:18:03.580
mentor, or even just a really
dope team lead that somehow

00:18:03.580 --> 00:18:07.000
made your work amazing, and you
were somehow kicking out way

00:18:07.000 --> 00:18:08.920
more work than you
ever did before?

00:18:08.920 --> 00:18:09.790
Yeah.

00:18:09.790 --> 00:18:10.870
So this makes sense.

00:18:10.870 --> 00:18:14.920
Because their influence
flows through, and magnifies,

00:18:14.920 --> 00:18:16.210
and amplifies all the work.

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:21.440
So it looks crazy.

00:18:21.440 --> 00:18:26.270
But it all does boil down
to culture, and tooling,

00:18:26.270 --> 00:18:28.150
and process.

00:18:28.150 --> 00:18:29.890
And we've got some
great evidence

00:18:29.890 --> 00:18:33.273
to help us point to
what to look for.

00:18:33.273 --> 00:18:34.690
Now again though,
sometimes people

00:18:34.690 --> 00:18:37.107
will look at this and they're
like, where do I even start?

00:18:37.107 --> 00:18:40.670
This is 37 things.

00:18:40.670 --> 00:18:43.430
Let's think back.

00:18:43.430 --> 00:18:45.940
Identify your constraint.

00:18:45.940 --> 00:18:49.890
What's your team's
biggest hurdle?

00:18:49.890 --> 00:18:52.170
Focus there.

00:18:52.170 --> 00:18:52.980
Start there.

00:18:55.720 --> 00:18:57.010
Eliminate that constraint.

00:18:57.010 --> 00:18:58.510
So in theory of
constraints, we call

00:18:58.510 --> 00:19:00.700
that elevating the constraint.

00:19:00.700 --> 00:19:04.020
Once it's no longer the
thing that's in your way,

00:19:04.020 --> 00:19:05.694
focus on the next constraint.

00:19:08.360 --> 00:19:10.260
Now, the next big
question I get is,

00:19:10.260 --> 00:19:14.000
how do I measure performance?

00:19:14.000 --> 00:19:16.960
The biggest mistakes I
often see are these--

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:21.790
focusing on outputs
versus outcomes,

00:19:21.790 --> 00:19:24.040
the difference between
individual and local measures

00:19:24.040 --> 00:19:26.892
versus team and global measures.

00:19:26.892 --> 00:19:28.600
So let's look through
some examples here.

00:19:31.530 --> 00:19:34.680
Lines of code-- this
is the best example

00:19:34.680 --> 00:19:36.405
I have for outputs
versus outcomes.

00:19:39.510 --> 00:19:42.047
It's funny, because sometimes
I bring this up as an example.

00:19:42.047 --> 00:19:43.880
And so often people are
like, lines of code?

00:19:43.880 --> 00:19:45.588
Who still gets asked
about lines of code?

00:19:45.588 --> 00:19:48.870
I get asked about lines of
code at least every month,

00:19:48.870 --> 00:19:50.720
almost every other week.

00:19:50.720 --> 00:19:54.270
So lines of code is dumb.

00:19:54.270 --> 00:19:55.560
And here's the best examples.

00:19:55.560 --> 00:19:57.420
Sometimes people are like,
well, more lines of code

00:19:57.420 --> 00:19:58.410
is going to be good.

00:19:58.410 --> 00:19:59.730
More lines of code is not good.

00:19:59.730 --> 00:20:00.960
Who already knows this?

00:20:00.960 --> 00:20:02.417
We already know.

00:20:02.417 --> 00:20:03.250
Y'all are my people.

00:20:03.250 --> 00:20:05.910
This is a smart room.

00:20:05.910 --> 00:20:09.150
More code just gives
us more to maintain.

00:20:09.150 --> 00:20:11.490
Bloated software,
slows things down,

00:20:11.490 --> 00:20:14.445
higher maintenance costs,
higher cost of change.

00:20:18.590 --> 00:20:19.550
Maybe less is better.

00:20:22.220 --> 00:20:25.910
But who has seen this ridiculous
thing where someone comes up

00:20:25.910 --> 00:20:35.660
with this amazingly
clever one line of code

00:20:35.660 --> 00:20:39.090
that, two weeks later, no one
can read, no one can interpret,

00:20:39.090 --> 00:20:42.430
we have no idea what happened?

00:20:42.430 --> 00:20:46.390
Sometimes the best answer
is to delete code, or change

00:20:46.390 --> 00:20:49.660
a business process so
that you delete code.

00:20:49.660 --> 00:20:54.060
Lines of code just is not
a great proxy for things.

00:20:54.060 --> 00:20:58.690
It's difficult.
That's an output.

00:20:58.690 --> 00:21:00.267
It's a measure of output.

00:21:00.267 --> 00:21:02.100
But it doesn't tell us
what we accomplished.

00:21:06.833 --> 00:21:09.000
By the way, that's why we
focus on software delivery

00:21:09.000 --> 00:21:09.630
performance.

00:21:09.630 --> 00:21:11.100
Because it's at the end.

00:21:11.100 --> 00:21:13.740
So let's talk about
velocity then.

00:21:13.740 --> 00:21:18.330
So for some background,
when we were in Agile land,

00:21:18.330 --> 00:21:20.520
we break problems
down into stories,

00:21:20.520 --> 00:21:22.950
which then we assign points,
and we estimate effort

00:21:22.950 --> 00:21:23.700
to complete these.

00:21:23.700 --> 00:21:25.620
We're all familiar
with this, right?

00:21:25.620 --> 00:21:27.000
OK, great.

00:21:27.000 --> 00:21:30.270
At the end of the
sprint, the total points

00:21:30.270 --> 00:21:31.870
are signed off by the customer.

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:33.420
This is velocity.

00:21:33.420 --> 00:21:36.390
By the way, velocity is
a capacity finding tool.

00:21:36.390 --> 00:21:40.920
We shouldn't be using this
to measure productivity.

00:21:40.920 --> 00:21:43.770
Over the years,
teams have often used

00:21:43.770 --> 00:21:46.380
this to measure productivity.

00:21:46.380 --> 00:21:48.960
But it's not great as a
measure of productivity.

00:21:48.960 --> 00:21:50.970
And here's why.

00:21:50.970 --> 00:21:52.930
Velocity is a relative measure.

00:21:52.930 --> 00:21:54.870
It's not an absolute measure.

00:21:54.870 --> 00:21:57.660
It's relative to the
team, to the tasks

00:21:57.660 --> 00:22:01.140
they're doing, to the context
that they're working in.

00:22:01.140 --> 00:22:04.020
We should never be comparing
teams on their velocity.

00:22:04.020 --> 00:22:06.690
We can also game it by
inflating estimates,

00:22:06.690 --> 00:22:08.280
which is understandable.

00:22:08.280 --> 00:22:10.770
We want to make sure
that we have our targets.

00:22:10.770 --> 00:22:13.590
This last point is the
most important though.

00:22:13.590 --> 00:22:16.930
We want to be, again,
thinking about outcomes.

00:22:16.930 --> 00:22:17.650
What's the goal?

00:22:17.650 --> 00:22:19.530
What are the team's working on?

00:22:19.530 --> 00:22:21.690
If I'm working on
a team, and I have

00:22:21.690 --> 00:22:23.065
points that I want
to accomplish,

00:22:23.065 --> 00:22:25.232
and you're working on a
team, and you have something

00:22:25.232 --> 00:22:26.640
that you want to
accomplish, what

00:22:26.640 --> 00:22:27.870
happens if there's
something that

00:22:27.870 --> 00:22:30.245
needs to be accomplished for
the entire organization that

00:22:30.245 --> 00:22:32.172
requires us working together?

00:22:32.172 --> 00:22:33.390
I'm not going to help you.

00:22:33.390 --> 00:22:36.670
I got points I got to finish.

00:22:36.670 --> 00:22:39.665
So we want to be thinking
about outcomes, not outputs.

00:22:43.750 --> 00:22:45.970
Finally, utilization.

00:22:45.970 --> 00:22:49.680
Utilization is good, but
only up and to a point.

00:22:49.680 --> 00:22:52.460
Because more isn't
necessarily better.

00:22:52.460 --> 00:22:55.010
It doesn't allow for slack.

00:22:55.010 --> 00:22:57.575
And we all know that we
always have unplanned work.

00:23:00.560 --> 00:23:03.240
And if anything, when people
come back to me and they

00:23:03.240 --> 00:23:06.960
debate this point, I'll
just point back to math.

00:23:06.960 --> 00:23:11.790
Queue theory tells me that as
utilization approaches 100%,

00:23:11.790 --> 00:23:15.000
lead times approach infinity.

00:23:18.430 --> 00:23:20.260
Once we get higher
and higher and higher

00:23:20.260 --> 00:23:22.750
levels of utilization,
your teams

00:23:22.750 --> 00:23:25.040
take longer to get work done.

00:23:25.040 --> 00:23:26.290
I can think about this myself.

00:23:26.290 --> 00:23:28.730
The longer my to do
list gets, the longer it

00:23:28.730 --> 00:23:29.980
takes me to get anything done.

00:23:35.560 --> 00:23:37.540
Why should we care
about any of this?

00:23:37.540 --> 00:23:38.300
Why do we care?

00:23:38.300 --> 00:23:41.680
Why do we care about
delivering software faster?

00:23:41.680 --> 00:23:46.090
Why do we care about it
being faster and more stable?

00:23:46.090 --> 00:23:47.880
Here's why.

00:23:47.880 --> 00:23:50.070
Teams that deliver
more often-- when

00:23:50.070 --> 00:23:52.800
I compare those elite performers
and those low performers,

00:23:52.800 --> 00:23:54.900
we see 46 times
more frequent code

00:23:54.900 --> 00:24:00.000
deployments, over 2,500 times
faster from commit to deploy,

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:04.260
seven times lower change fail
rate, and over 2,600 times

00:24:04.260 --> 00:24:08.020
faster time to recover
from incidents.

00:24:08.020 --> 00:24:11.140
It lets us get our features
to customers faster.

00:24:11.140 --> 00:24:14.150
It lets us recover
from downtime.

00:24:14.150 --> 00:24:16.360
It lets us patch faster.

00:24:16.360 --> 00:24:19.150
We can get out our compliance
and regulatory changes

00:24:19.150 --> 00:24:21.530
better and faster.

00:24:21.530 --> 00:24:26.470
It also makes it just easier
and safer to push our code.

00:24:26.470 --> 00:24:29.220
It lets us get back to the fun
part of what it is that we do.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.450
It also helps us contribute
to the organization,

00:24:37.450 --> 00:24:41.630
whether that means money,
commercial goals, productivity,

00:24:41.630 --> 00:24:46.220
profitability, market share,
or non-commercial goals.

00:24:46.220 --> 00:24:49.400
Because we care about more
than just the bottom line.

00:24:49.400 --> 00:24:51.990
We care about things like
effectiveness, efficiency,

00:24:51.990 --> 00:24:54.087
and customer satisfaction.

00:24:54.087 --> 00:24:55.670
And I really love
the shift that we've

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:58.550
been seeing in the
broader industry

00:24:58.550 --> 00:25:00.140
over the last several
years to things

00:25:00.140 --> 00:25:02.930
like corporate social
responsibility.

00:25:02.930 --> 00:25:06.680
And we know that
delivering software better

00:25:06.680 --> 00:25:08.090
helps us achieve these goals.

00:25:11.690 --> 00:25:15.280
For bonus points, we also
see that elite performers--

00:25:15.280 --> 00:25:19.360
the highest performers see
50% higher market cap growth

00:25:19.360 --> 00:25:21.610
over the previous three years.

00:25:21.610 --> 00:25:23.650
Now with a little
asterisk, that analysis

00:25:23.650 --> 00:25:25.487
was only available
for those companies

00:25:25.487 --> 00:25:27.820
that are publicly traded on
the New York Stock Exchange.

00:25:30.990 --> 00:25:33.130
Now why else do we care?

00:25:33.130 --> 00:25:36.550
This also contributes
to quality.

00:25:36.550 --> 00:25:41.070
We see high performers are
able to do more new work.

00:25:41.070 --> 00:25:44.010
Now, what do I mean by new work?

00:25:44.010 --> 00:25:50.450
They don't have to do as much
rework or unplanned work.

00:25:50.450 --> 00:25:53.630
We get to do more novel
work, more fun development.

00:25:53.630 --> 00:25:57.920
Now, when I see development,
I don't just mean developers.

00:25:57.920 --> 00:25:59.420
I was a sysadmin for years.

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:02.050
Sometimes I want to
create new infrastructure.

00:26:02.050 --> 00:26:05.690
I want to do more
creating of whatever it

00:26:05.690 --> 00:26:08.350
is that I'm building.

00:26:08.350 --> 00:26:10.005
That's new work as well.

00:26:10.005 --> 00:26:12.130
I don't want to have to
keep fixing stuff that I've

00:26:12.130 --> 00:26:12.630
done before.

00:26:16.530 --> 00:26:19.590
This was the part that
matters so much to me.

00:26:19.590 --> 00:26:24.750
It also helps us
make our work better.

00:26:24.750 --> 00:26:26.125
It contributes to
happier people.

00:26:28.970 --> 00:26:31.410
Because we don't just want
to burn out all the time.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:33.710
The thing I love is that
the promise of DevOps

00:26:33.710 --> 00:26:36.080
makes work better.

00:26:36.080 --> 00:26:38.390
It decreases burnout.

00:26:38.390 --> 00:26:41.210
And by the way, burnout
isn't just being tired.

00:26:41.210 --> 00:26:44.268
Burnout is when we
don't love what we used

00:26:44.268 --> 00:26:45.560
to love anymore about our work.

00:26:48.410 --> 00:26:52.400
Making smart investments
in tech and process

00:26:52.400 --> 00:26:53.915
actually combats this.

00:26:58.660 --> 00:27:00.850
It also helps
decrease deploy pain.

00:27:00.850 --> 00:27:02.930
That's that panic when
you push into prod,

00:27:02.930 --> 00:27:05.280
and you're like,
please just work.

00:27:05.280 --> 00:27:08.530
You know that awful
meme that's hilarious,

00:27:08.530 --> 00:27:10.280
but it's funny
because it's true,

00:27:10.280 --> 00:27:12.190
with the girl in
the burning house?

00:27:12.190 --> 00:27:13.480
It worked fine in DevOps.

00:27:13.480 --> 00:27:14.140
Problem now.

00:27:19.690 --> 00:27:22.030
It helps get rid of that.

00:27:22.030 --> 00:27:25.300
Also, it gives us
better job satisfaction.

00:27:25.300 --> 00:27:28.420
And when I say job satisfaction,
our research into job

00:27:28.420 --> 00:27:30.410
satisfaction wasn't
just, I like my work.

00:27:30.410 --> 00:27:34.400
It's, my work makes good use
of my skills and abilities.

00:27:34.400 --> 00:27:37.240
And I have the tools
I need to do my job.

00:27:37.240 --> 00:27:39.330
So it's like, I get
to do dope stuff.

00:27:42.100 --> 00:27:45.360
We also see higher Net
Promoter score from employees.

00:27:45.360 --> 00:27:47.070
So they love what
they do, and they

00:27:47.070 --> 00:27:49.290
tell everyone else about it.

00:27:49.290 --> 00:27:52.430
And a better
organizational culture.

00:27:52.430 --> 00:27:56.440
Speaking of-- I keep
saying "our culture".

00:27:56.440 --> 00:27:59.650
Who here has a definition
of our culture?

00:27:59.650 --> 00:28:01.985
Who here has three
definitions of our culture?

00:28:01.985 --> 00:28:03.610
Who here is sick of
raising their hand?

00:28:07.360 --> 00:28:09.780
We always talk about
organizational culture

00:28:09.780 --> 00:28:10.980
when we talk about DevOps.

00:28:10.980 --> 00:28:13.980
And I think it's
because, for years, we

00:28:13.980 --> 00:28:16.230
were doing this
technology thing,

00:28:16.230 --> 00:28:20.840
and promising it would
make a difference.

00:28:20.840 --> 00:28:24.080
And to be frank, it never did.

00:28:24.080 --> 00:28:27.270
And when I started
doing this research,

00:28:27.270 --> 00:28:28.650
people told me I was crazy.

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:31.500
I actually almost got kicked
out of my PhD program.

00:28:31.500 --> 00:28:33.210
Because for 40 years,
we had research

00:28:33.210 --> 00:28:36.560
showing that investments in
technology didn't matter.

00:28:36.560 --> 00:28:38.350
Have we all heard this whole--

00:28:38.350 --> 00:28:41.080
IT is a cost center?

00:28:41.080 --> 00:28:43.435
That sucks.

00:28:43.435 --> 00:28:44.060
But here's why.

00:28:44.060 --> 00:28:46.600
It was because we used to
just buy tech, and then

00:28:46.600 --> 00:28:51.525
walk away, and expect the
magical fairy to show up.

00:28:51.525 --> 00:28:53.400
The problem is that if
anyone else could just

00:28:53.400 --> 00:28:56.700
buy tech and walk away
and do nothing else,

00:28:56.700 --> 00:28:58.740
that doesn't make you
magically better than all

00:28:58.740 --> 00:28:59.573
of your competitors.

00:28:59.573 --> 00:29:03.840
It doesn't help you make things
awesome for your customer

00:29:03.840 --> 00:29:05.940
or your end user.

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:08.490
What we have to do is,
we have to find ways

00:29:08.490 --> 00:29:10.530
to differentiate ourselves.

00:29:10.530 --> 00:29:14.550
And that requires
technology, and process,

00:29:14.550 --> 00:29:16.500
and culture, and people.

00:29:16.500 --> 00:29:20.030
And then the magical
fairy shows up.

00:29:20.030 --> 00:29:21.940
And so culture is
really, really important.

00:29:21.940 --> 00:29:23.550
Because that's
what allows people

00:29:23.550 --> 00:29:29.990
who are fundamentally different
and diverse in background,

00:29:29.990 --> 00:29:36.820
and technology, and skill set
to talk together and make these

00:29:36.820 --> 00:29:39.700
incredibly complex,
diverse distributed systems

00:29:39.700 --> 00:29:44.740
work in these ways that
we've never seen before.

00:29:44.740 --> 00:29:47.830
And so when we started
doing this work,

00:29:47.830 --> 00:29:50.670
we reached back into some
of the other literature

00:29:50.670 --> 00:29:54.090
and found this great
research by a sociologist

00:29:54.090 --> 00:29:56.580
named Ron Westrum.

00:29:56.580 --> 00:30:03.660
Now, he was doing research
into high risk complex systems,

00:30:03.660 --> 00:30:09.200
where if something breaks,
real bad things happen.

00:30:09.200 --> 00:30:15.730
So I'm talking health care,
nuclear power, aviation.

00:30:15.730 --> 00:30:19.780
Notice that there, when bad
things happen, people die,

00:30:19.780 --> 00:30:20.930
immediately.

00:30:20.930 --> 00:30:28.050
Now to be fair, that's
also kind of true in tech.

00:30:28.050 --> 00:30:30.360
Because we're also
working in systems

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:34.770
that power health care, and
aviation, and these very, very

00:30:34.770 --> 00:30:35.910
high risk systems.

00:30:35.910 --> 00:30:39.660
I remember pushing into
prod, and we almost

00:30:39.660 --> 00:30:42.270
had to evacuate an ICU
when a backup failed,

00:30:42.270 --> 00:30:44.110
and it failed horribly.

00:30:44.110 --> 00:30:46.500
So we've also had these
situations happen,

00:30:46.500 --> 00:30:48.780
where something bad happens
and our stomachs knot.

00:30:52.480 --> 00:30:55.960
So this organizational
culture maps up

00:30:55.960 --> 00:30:57.850
to so much of what
we see when we

00:30:57.850 --> 00:31:03.640
talk about having a culture
that values information

00:31:03.640 --> 00:31:10.670
flow, and transparency,
and learning from failure,

00:31:10.670 --> 00:31:12.095
and bridging across silos.

00:31:15.730 --> 00:31:17.230
And so when we
found this, we were

00:31:17.230 --> 00:31:22.270
like, this might be a great
way to talk about, and measure,

00:31:22.270 --> 00:31:24.400
and think about
a DevOps culture.

00:31:24.400 --> 00:31:27.020
Is this resonating?

00:31:27.020 --> 00:31:29.440
OK, so let's look about
the pathological one--

00:31:29.440 --> 00:31:30.370
power oriented.

00:31:30.370 --> 00:31:33.500
It's typified by
low cooperation.

00:31:33.500 --> 00:31:34.500
We shoot the messenger.

00:31:34.500 --> 00:31:36.470
We don't want to hear bad news.

00:31:36.470 --> 00:31:39.060
Responsibilities are shirked.

00:31:39.060 --> 00:31:41.190
Bridging is discouraged.

00:31:41.190 --> 00:31:43.560
Failure scapegoating,
novelties crushed.

00:31:43.560 --> 00:31:48.690
Who here has a friend
that works here?

00:31:48.690 --> 00:31:50.730
OK, a couple of friends here.

00:31:50.730 --> 00:31:54.870
Across our research, we've
seen about 25%-30% fall here.

00:31:54.870 --> 00:31:57.450
And it's highly correlated
with low performers.

00:31:57.450 --> 00:31:59.645
Bureaucratic is
very rule oriented.

00:31:59.645 --> 00:32:01.770
Now to be fair, this isn't
necessarily a bad thing.

00:32:01.770 --> 00:32:07.080
Bureaucratic cultures are
meant to optimize for fairness.

00:32:07.080 --> 00:32:10.350
Modest cooperation among these
very, very different teams--

00:32:10.350 --> 00:32:11.460
messengers are neglected.

00:32:11.460 --> 00:32:14.670
If someone brings me bad news,
I just kind of ignore it.

00:32:14.670 --> 00:32:16.470
I don't want to hear it.

00:32:16.470 --> 00:32:18.770
Narrowly defined
responsibilities.

00:32:18.770 --> 00:32:20.670
Bridging is tolerated.

00:32:20.670 --> 00:32:23.100
Failure leads to justice.

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:25.230
Novelty leads to problems.

00:32:25.230 --> 00:32:27.790
Who here has a friend
who works here?

00:32:27.790 --> 00:32:28.290
OK.

00:32:28.290 --> 00:32:29.165
We've got some hands.

00:32:29.165 --> 00:32:32.400
I want to say about 55%
of respondents in here,

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:36.270
and it's highly correlated
with medium of performance.

00:32:36.270 --> 00:32:38.690
Bridging-- when we
bridge across teams--

00:32:38.690 --> 00:32:41.910
so Dev bridges to Ops
bridges to testing,

00:32:41.910 --> 00:32:44.480
or even bridges to something
like marketing-- the question

00:32:44.480 --> 00:32:47.550
is, what do I mean by bridging?

00:32:47.550 --> 00:32:50.100
Now, the final one--
generative or performance

00:32:50.100 --> 00:32:50.980
oriented culture.

00:32:50.980 --> 00:32:52.290
High cooperation.

00:32:52.290 --> 00:32:55.170
High cooperation among
teams that are traditionally

00:32:55.170 --> 00:32:56.640
very different.

00:32:56.640 --> 00:32:58.050
Messengers are trained.

00:32:58.050 --> 00:33:00.210
So what do I mean by this?

00:33:00.210 --> 00:33:02.520
I want people to
bring me bad news,

00:33:02.520 --> 00:33:05.280
because then when I know
it, then I can fix it.

00:33:05.280 --> 00:33:07.810
Risks are shared.

00:33:07.810 --> 00:33:10.210
Bridging is encouraged.

00:33:10.210 --> 00:33:12.810
Failure leads to inquiry.

00:33:12.810 --> 00:33:16.540
When I read that, I think
of blameless postmortems.

00:33:16.540 --> 00:33:19.650
And the novelty is implemented.

00:33:19.650 --> 00:33:21.570
Who here has a friend,
or is lucky enough

00:33:21.570 --> 00:33:23.950
to work somewhere like this?

00:33:23.950 --> 00:33:24.950
Who here has no friends?

00:33:30.790 --> 00:33:34.150
I love this, because
it mirrors some

00:33:34.150 --> 00:33:37.780
of the research done by
Google's teams a few years ago

00:33:37.780 --> 00:33:40.605
from project Aristotle.

00:33:40.605 --> 00:33:42.230
The background to
some of this research

00:33:42.230 --> 00:33:44.770
was, they were wondering what
would make the perfect team.

00:33:44.770 --> 00:33:46.537
And if I recall
correctly, I want

00:33:46.537 --> 00:33:48.120
to say the hypothesis
moving into this

00:33:48.120 --> 00:33:51.290
was that, the perfect team would
have the perfect mix of skills,

00:33:51.290 --> 00:33:53.300
like technical skills.

00:33:53.300 --> 00:33:57.230
What they found instead was
that the number one driver

00:33:57.230 --> 00:33:59.300
was psychological safety.

00:33:59.300 --> 00:34:02.870
They feel safe to take risks
and be vulnerable in front

00:34:02.870 --> 00:34:06.950
of each other, followed by
dependability, structure

00:34:06.950 --> 00:34:09.765
and clarity, meaning and impact.

00:34:12.429 --> 00:34:15.310
It comes down to team dynamics.

00:34:15.310 --> 00:34:18.820
It comes down to the culture.

00:34:18.820 --> 00:34:21.279
That's what really
drives impacts in teams.

00:34:25.360 --> 00:34:27.219
And I think it's
particularly interesting,

00:34:27.219 --> 00:34:29.800
because as I refer
back to Westrum's

00:34:29.800 --> 00:34:32.830
organizational culture, the
thing that was particularly

00:34:32.830 --> 00:34:38.650
interesting is, in addition
to defining and predicting

00:34:38.650 --> 00:34:41.409
outcomes, it also
helped explain what

00:34:41.409 --> 00:34:44.730
happens when things go wrong.

00:34:44.730 --> 00:34:46.159
How often do systems fail?

00:34:46.159 --> 00:34:47.239
All the time.

00:34:47.239 --> 00:34:50.670
Because we're working with
highly, highly complex systems.

00:34:50.670 --> 00:34:54.710
So what else we
see is that failure

00:34:54.710 --> 00:34:57.770
in these complex systems
will always happen.

00:34:57.770 --> 00:35:02.790
There's no way to guarantee
against all failure.

00:35:02.790 --> 00:35:06.270
So in these systems we
often see that human error

00:35:06.270 --> 00:35:09.120
is the starting point.

00:35:09.120 --> 00:35:11.420
And so when we do
blameless postmortems,

00:35:11.420 --> 00:35:14.120
we want to understand
where to move forward.

00:35:14.120 --> 00:35:18.680
So how can we get people
better information?

00:35:18.680 --> 00:35:22.640
And then how can we detect
and limit failure modes?

00:35:22.640 --> 00:35:24.960
How can we build
our systems better?

00:35:27.700 --> 00:35:31.600
I love this picture
on this example.

00:35:31.600 --> 00:35:35.650
Ryn Daniels was at
Etsy for a while.

00:35:35.650 --> 00:35:37.880
Is anyone familiar
with this story?

00:35:37.880 --> 00:35:38.890
This story is dope.

00:35:38.890 --> 00:35:42.880
So at Etsy, they
actually reward people

00:35:42.880 --> 00:35:47.210
for finding fantastic
failures in the system.

00:35:47.210 --> 00:35:49.470
And why is this?

00:35:49.470 --> 00:35:54.080
It's because you have
uncovered an opportunity

00:35:54.080 --> 00:35:56.900
to build more resiliency
into the system.

00:35:56.900 --> 00:36:02.660
So Ryn here was awarded
the three-armed sweater.

00:36:02.660 --> 00:36:04.210
Are we familiar with Etsy?

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:05.980
Etsy is a marketplace
where users

00:36:05.980 --> 00:36:08.980
can sell their handmade goods.

00:36:08.980 --> 00:36:12.340
One of their users knitted
them a three-armed sweater.

00:36:12.340 --> 00:36:16.360
And so every year, they
have an award show.

00:36:16.360 --> 00:36:20.100
And Ryn writes about
this on a blog.

00:36:20.100 --> 00:36:22.730
And it's on failure
and resilience.

00:36:22.730 --> 00:36:25.740
And it really is an honor.

00:36:25.740 --> 00:36:28.930
And it's very unintentional,
but the better

00:36:28.930 --> 00:36:31.510
you can bring down
the system, it

00:36:31.510 --> 00:36:35.000
uncovers fantastic
opportunities for them.

00:36:35.000 --> 00:36:38.510
And so this is where we're
Ryn wins and is awarded

00:36:38.510 --> 00:36:39.510
the three-armed sweater.

00:36:43.110 --> 00:36:48.780
And Kripa Krishnan talks about
this at Cloud Operations.

00:36:48.780 --> 00:36:52.530
For DiRT Operations-- so DiRT
is disaster resilience testing.

00:36:52.530 --> 00:36:55.290
For DiRT style events
to be successful,

00:36:55.290 --> 00:37:00.480
an organization first needs
to accept system and process

00:37:00.480 --> 00:37:04.290
failures as a means of learning.

00:37:04.290 --> 00:37:10.630
Remember back how failure
was an opportunity to learn?

00:37:10.630 --> 00:37:12.730
We design tests that
require engineers

00:37:12.730 --> 00:37:15.700
from several groups who might
not normally work together

00:37:15.700 --> 00:37:17.440
to interact with each other.

00:37:17.440 --> 00:37:21.400
That way, should a real large
scale disaster ever strike,

00:37:21.400 --> 00:37:25.210
these people will already have
strong working relationships.

00:37:25.210 --> 00:37:26.680
I love that as well--

00:37:26.680 --> 00:37:29.530
bridging across teams,
breaking down silos.

00:37:34.340 --> 00:37:41.210
So in conclusion, direction
matters more than maturity.

00:37:41.210 --> 00:37:43.580
I know everyone loves
to benchmark themselves

00:37:43.580 --> 00:37:47.950
against where they are, and
know what level we're on.

00:37:47.950 --> 00:37:50.240
But we always need to
keep getting better.

00:37:50.240 --> 00:37:52.090
What happens if we
get to level five,

00:37:52.090 --> 00:37:54.610
and a few years later, suddenly
the industry has magically

00:37:54.610 --> 00:37:56.800
decided that we get
to a level eight?

00:37:56.800 --> 00:38:01.100
We just need to keep moving
forward and getting better.

00:38:01.100 --> 00:38:06.700
Second, we can have it all, or
at least speed and stability.

00:38:06.700 --> 00:38:10.330
DevOps culture and practices
have a measurable impact

00:38:10.330 --> 00:38:13.570
on performance,
quality, and culture.

00:38:13.570 --> 00:38:15.955
And culture can be
measured and changed.

00:38:19.480 --> 00:38:23.560
One more plug-- if any of
this research or information

00:38:23.560 --> 00:38:27.280
was interesting to
you at all, the data

00:38:27.280 --> 00:38:30.700
collection for the 2019
State of DevOps report

00:38:30.700 --> 00:38:35.100
is open right now, and
will be open until May 3rd.

